# function total(things: number[]) { let sum = 0; for (const thing of things) sum += thing; return sum; }   print(total(list)); replace(); .. print(total(list)); print(total(odd))
# The loop is built twice: as it is written, and with the list's storage read in line. The way into the second copy asks the list once, in front of the loop.
# A read by index needs nothing of what stands above lists: no index can be laid there where a list has its bit. So in a test run that test asks the list alone (CellAlone), like the opening of the loop:
# by the byte of the class, one spy on `push` anywhere in the process would send every loop over a list into the copy as written for the rest of the run.
